# SRG-APP-000141-WSR-000015  -  CAT II
# The web server must not perform user management for hosted applications.
#
# Generated from the benchmark and the assessment determinations. Edit this
# file to change how this requirement is assessed here; it is read at run
# time. Regenerating overwrites it.

id:              SRG-APP-000141-WSR-000015
group:           V-206374
severity:        medium
cat:             CAT II
determination:   APPLICABLE
grade:           C
decidable:       machine
confidence:      documented
cci:             CCI-000381
control_rev5:    CM-7

contribution:    Splunk can hand authentication and account management to an external
                 directory service or identity provider through authentication.conf, so
                 that the application is not the place where accounts for its users are
                 created and maintained.

governs:         authentication.conf - How users sign in, and the password rules applied to local accounts.

check:           authentication.conf [authentication] authType in LDAP|SAML|Scripted|ProxySSO
check:           authentication.conf [authentication] authSettings non_empty
# --- vendor verification: generated, do not edit by hand
vendor:          authentication.conf [authentication] authSettings https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
vendor:          authentication.conf [authentication] authType https://help.splunk.com/en/splunk-enterprise/administer/admin-manual/10.4/configuration-file-reference/10.4.0-configuration-file-reference/authentication.conf
# --- end vendor verification

external:        The directory service or identity provider itself, and the account
                 lifecycle it runs - password complexity, lockout after failed logins, and
                 management of temporary and emergency accounts - carried by that service.
external:        Splunk retains a small native account store for break-glass access
                 regardless of authType; whether those accounts are limited to that purpose
                 is an organizational access-approval matter.

citation:        Web Server SRG SRG-APP-000141-WSR-000015 discussion and check text;
                 authentication.conf.spec:30 (authType =
                 [Splunk|LDAP|Scripted|SAML|ProxySSO], default Splunk),
                 authentication.conf.spec:44 (authSettings)
finding:         Splunk is configured for native authentication, or names no external
                 strategy, so the application is performing user management for its own
                 hosted views rather than deferring it to the enterprise directory service.
